Task 17 — Active Users / Presence: Pre-Implementation Report

1. Current WebSocket/Session Architecture

JwtChannelInterceptor (realtime/JwtChannelInterceptor.java):
- Intercepts only CONNECT frames
- Validates JWT from Authorization: Bearer <token> header using JwtDecoder
- On success: calls accessor.setUser(new JwtAuthenticationToken(jwt, Collections.emptyList())) — the JwtAuthenticationToken.getName() returns the JWT sub claim, which is the userId UUID string
- On failure: throws BadCredentialsException → STOMP ERROR frame sent → WebSocket closed → SessionConnectedEvent is never fired for rejected connections

WebSocketConfig (realtime/WebSocketConfig.java):
- Simple broker on /topic
- STOMP endpoint: /ws (no SockJS)
- JwtChannelInterceptor registered as inbound channel interceptor

AreaEventRelay (realtime/AreaEventRelay.java):
- @TransactionalEventListener(AFTER_COMMIT) publishes to /topic/workspace/{workspaceId} via SimpMessagingTemplate

WebSocketSessionMetrics (metrics/WebSocketSessionMetrics.java):
- @EventListener on SessionConnectedEvent → adds sessionId to ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()
- @EventListener on SessionDisconnectEvent → removes sessionId (idempotent)
- SessionConnectedEvent exposes: StompHeaderAccessor.wrap(event.getMessage()).getSessionId()
- SessionDisconnectEvent exposes: event.getSessionId()

SessionConnectedEvent carries the Principal: AbstractSubProtocolEvent.getUser() returns the JwtAuthenticationToken set by JwtChannelInterceptor, so event.getUser().getName() is the userId UUID string. This is confirmed by the Spring STOMP lifecycle — the user Principal from CONNECT is associated with the session and propagated to subsequent lifecycle events.

SessionDisconnectEvent — safe assumption for Principal: Spring propagates the session Principal to disconnect events via the SimpMessageHeaderAccessor.USER_HEADER. However, to avoid any uncertainty, the PresenceService should maintain a reverse sessionId → userId map populated at connect time, rather than relying on event.getUser() at disconnect time.

JWT payload (JwtIssuer.java:30): Contains only sub = userId.toString(), iss, iat, exp. No user name is in the JWT. Name resolution requires a UserRepository lookup.

Prototype workspace: PrototypeProperties.workspaceId() is the single workspace UUID (00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002). All registered users are added as EDITORs of this workspace at registration time (AuthService.register()). The frontend hardcodes this same UUID in environment.workspaceId.

Frontend AuthService (auth/auth.service.ts): Stores only the JWT token in localStorage. The user's {id, name} from the AuthResponse is discarded after login/register. No current mechanism exists to identify the logged-in user's name on the frontend without re-parsing the auth response.

2. Definition of "Active User"

Active user = authenticated user with at least one currently established STOMP/WebSocket session.

Specifically: a user for whom SessionConnectedEvent has fired (STOMP handshake complete) and for whom no final SessionDisconnectEvent has yet fired for all their sessions.

Not counted:
- Users with a valid JWT in localStorage but no active WebSocket connection
- Users who have made recent REST requests
- Users whose CONNECT was rejected (bad/expired JWT)

3. Attach at CONNECT or SUBSCRIBE?

Attach at CONNECT (SessionConnectedEvent), not at SUBSCRIBE.

Reasoning: In the current architecture, JwtChannelInterceptor ensures that every SessionConnectedEvent represents an authenticated session. The prototype has exactly one workspace, and the frontend immediately subscribes to /topic/workspace/{workspaceId} in its onConnect callback — the time between CONNECT and SUBSCRIBE is milliseconds and never crosses an authentication boundary.

SUBSCRIBE-based presence would require parsing the destination path (e.g., /topic/workspace/00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000002) to extract workspaceId, and listening to SessionSubscribeEvent. This adds complexity for no benefit in a single-workspace prototype.

Workspace identity at CONNECT time: Since all authenticated sessions belong to the prototype workspace, the PresenceService uses PrototypeProperties.workspaceId() as the implicit workspace ID for all presence state. This is a documented prototype simplification; production multi-workspace presence would need workspace resolution at SUBSCRIBE time.

4. How workspaceId is Determined

PrototypeProperties.workspaceId() — injected into PresenceService and PresenceController. Not derived from the CONNECT or SUBSCRIBE frame. All presence state is implicitly scoped to the single prototype workspace.

5. How userId and User Name are Resolved

- userId: event.getUser().getName() on SessionConnectedEvent → JWT sub → UUID string → parsed as UUID
- name: UserRepository.findById(userId) at connect time → User.getName()
- One DB read per STOMP CONNECT event (infrequent; acceptable)
- If user not found (edge case: orphaned JWT for deleted user), presence is not registered for that session; the connect is allowed to succeed (presence is best-effort)
- Email: not exposed in presence data; name is sufficient for the map UI

6. Recommended In-Memory Data Structure

// Primary state: userId → set of active sessionIds
ConcurrentHashMap<String, Set<String>> userSessions;
// where each Set is ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()

// Reverse lookup: sessionId → userId (for disconnect)
ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> sessionToUser;

// Name cache: userId → name (populated at connect, cleaned at last disconnect)
ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> userNames;

The reverse map avoids any reliance on the Principal being available in SessionDisconnectEvent. Both maps are ConcurrentHashMap for thread-safe concurrent connect/disconnect.

On CONNECT (atomic via ConcurrentHashMap.compute):
1. userSessions.compute(userId, (k, sessions) -> { ... sessions.add(sessionId); ... }) — detect if this is the first session (null entry)
2. sessionToUser.put(sessionId, userId)
3. userNames.put(userId, name) (idempotent for subsequent sessions)
4. If first session for user: broadcast PRESENCE_JOINED

On DISCONNECT (atomic via computeIfPresent):
1. Look up userId = sessionToUser.remove(sessionId)
2. userSessions.computeIfPresent(userId, (k, sessions) -> { sessions.remove(sessionId); return sessions.isEmpty() ? null : sessions; }) — atomic removal; null return removes the entry
3. String removedName = result_was_null ? userNames.remove(userId) : null
4. If entry was removed (last session): broadcast PRESENCE_LEFT

This is correct under concurrent access. The compute/computeIfPresent operations on ConcurrentHashMap are atomic.

7. Multi-Tab / Device Semantics

Alice tab 1 CONNECT → userSessions.get("alice-id") = {session-1}  → PRESENCE_JOINED broadcast
Alice tab 2 CONNECT → userSessions.get("alice-id") = {session-1, session-2}  → no broadcast
Alice tab 1 DISCONNECT → userSessions.get("alice-id") = {session-2}  → no broadcast (still active)
Alice tab 2 DISCONNECT → userSessions.get("alice-id") = null (removed)  → PRESENCE_LEFT broadcast

Alice appears in the presence list exactly once throughout. The UI shows name (not sessionId) so multi-tab is transparent to other users.

8. Connect/Disconnect Lifecycle

Client STOMP CONNECT
  → JwtChannelInterceptor validates JWT, sets Principal
  → STOMP CONNECTED frame sent to client
  → Spring fires SessionConnectedEvent
  → PresenceService.join(sessionId, userId, name)
    → if first session for userId: broadcast PRESENCE_JOINED to /topic/workspace/{id}/presence

Client STOMP DISCONNECT (graceful)
  → Spring fires SessionDisconnectEvent
  → PresenceService.leave(sessionId)
    → look up userId from sessionToUser
    → remove sessionId from userSessions[userId]
    → if set now empty: remove entry, broadcast PRESENCE_LEFT to /topic/workspace/{id}/presence

9. Abnormal Disconnect Behavior

Spring SimpleBroker uses heartbeats to detect dead connections. When a connection is lost abruptly (network failure, crash, browser tab closed), the STOMP heartbeat timeout causes the session to be closed server-side. This fires SessionDisconnectEvent with closeStatus = ABNORMAL_CLOSURE or similar. The PresenceService.leave() path is the same regardless of close reason.

Result: Abnormal disconnects are handled correctly via the existing SessionDisconnectEvent mechanism. No grace period or heartbeat lease infrastructure is needed. The brief window between network loss and heartbeat timeout means a user may appear active for a few seconds after abnormal disconnect — acceptable for a prototype.

10. Duplicate Disconnect Handling

SessionDisconnectEvent may fire more than once for the same session (confirmed by WebSocketSessionMetrics javadoc). The PresenceService handles this correctly:
- sessionToUser.remove(sessionId) returns null on the second call (sessionId already removed)
- computeIfPresent on a non-existent key is a no-op
- Result: second disconnect for same session → no state change, no duplicate PRESENCE_LEFT broadcast

11. Authentication Failure Behavior

When JwtChannelInterceptor rejects a CONNECT (bad/expired JWT):
- Spring sends STOMP ERROR frame to client
- WebSocket connection closes
- SessionConnectedEvent is never fired (confirmed by WebSocketSessionMetrics javadoc)
- SessionDisconnectEvent may fire for the closed connection; since no entry was added to sessionToUser, PresenceService.leave() is a no-op

Result: rejected connections never appear in presence. ✓

12. Initial Presence Snapshot Mechanism

Recommendation: REST endpoint.

GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/presence
Authorization: Bearer <token>
→ 200 [{"id": "...", "name": "Alice"}, {"id": "...", "name": "Bob"}]

Rationale: fits the existing REST-authoritative pattern (same design as area CRUD). Simple to implement, test, and consume. No new STOMP request/reply infrastructure needed.

The frontend sequence after STOMP connect:
1. onConnect fires → subscribe to /topic/workspace/{id} (area events) and /topic/workspace/{id}/presence (presence events)
2. Call GET /workspaces/{workspaceId}/presence via REST → populate activeUsers map
3. Merge any PRESENCE_JOINED/PRESENCE_LEFT events received since subscription was established (handled idempotently by user ID key)

This ordering (subscribe before REST call) ensures no PRESENCE_JOINED events are missed between subscription and snapshot fetch. Any PRESENCE_JOINED event arriving for a user already in the snapshot is idempotent (map.set overwrites same value).

13. Realtime Presence Event Contract

Events are sent to a separate topic: /topic/workspace/{workspaceId}/presence

This avoids mixing presence events with area events on the existing topic and prevents the existing area event handler from receiving presence messages.

{
  "type": "PRESENCE_JOINED",
  "user": { "id": "uuid-string", "name": "Alice" }
}

{
  "type": "PRESENCE_LEFT",
  "user": { "id": "uuid-string", "name": "Alice" }
}

Java record:
record PresenceEventMessage(String type, UserPresenceResponse user) {}
record UserPresenceResponse(String id, String name) {}

PRESENCE_JOINED is broadcast when a user's first session connects.
PRESENCE_LEFT is broadcast when a user's last session disconnects.

14. Snapshot/Event Race Handling

The frontend stores active users in Map<string, UserPresence> keyed by userId (not an array). All updates are idempotent:
- PRESENCE_JOINED for a user already in the map: map.set(id, user) — no duplicate
- PRESENCE_LEFT for a user not in the map: map.delete(id) — no-op
- REST snapshot: replace map with fresh data; subsequent events applied on top

No sequence numbers or event ordering infrastructure is needed.

15. Task 16 Reconnect Interaction

Task 16 auto-reconnects with 3 s delay. Presence interaction:

Alice DISCONNECT (network loss)
  → SessionDisconnectEvent fires (after heartbeat timeout ~10 s)
  → Alice leaves presence
  → other clients receive PRESENCE_LEFT

  [Task 16 reconnects]

Alice CONNECT (reconnect succeeds)
  → SessionConnectedEvent fires
  → Alice re-joins presence
  → other clients receive PRESENCE_JOINED

Alice's own browser
  → onReconnect() callback fires → loadViewport() + loadPresence()
  → REST snapshot repopulates Alice's active-users list

A brief leave/join transition is expected and acceptable. No grace period is needed.

After reconnect, the onReconnect callback (already used for loadViewport()) should also call loadPresence() to refresh the presence snapshot. This handles the race where the snapshot could be stale after a prolonged disconnect.

16. Frontend State Model

In MapComponent:

activeUsers = new Map<string, UserPresence>(); // userId → {id, name}

Updates:
- loadPresence() — REST call → replaces map contents
- PRESENCE_JOINED event → activeUsers.set(user.id, user)
- PRESENCE_LEFT event → activeUsers.delete(user.id)

Map is the correct structure: O(1) lookup, automatic deduplication by userId, idempotent updates.

On ngOnDestroy: realtimeService.disconnect() is already called; no presence cleanup needed on the frontend (the server handles it via SessionDisconnectEvent).

